              "text": "You are likely looking for **Iuka, Mississippi** (with a capital **I**, though in many fonts the lowercase \"l\" and capital \"I\" look identical, leading to the common misspelling \"Luka\"). \n\nPronounced **\"eye-YOO-kuh\"**, this historic town is located in the far northeast corner of the state and serves as the county seat of **Tishomingo County**. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Geography and Surprising Elevation\nUnlike most of Mississippi, which is low-lying and flat, Iuka is situated among the scenic hills and valleys of the Tennessee River Valley. \n* **Woodall Mountain:** Located just south of Iuka, Woodall Mountain is the **highest natural point in Mississippi**, standing at 806 feet above sea level. \n* **The Climate:** Because of its elevation and surrounding Appalachian foothill forests, the area experiences slightly cooler, more temperate weather compared to the rest of the state.\n\n### 2. Historical Significance\n* **Origin of the Name:** The city was founded in 1857 along the Memphis and Charleston Railroad and was named after **Chief Iuka**, a revered chief of the Chickasaw Nation.\n* **The Battle of Iuka (September 19, 1862):** Iuka was the site of a major American Civil War battle. Union forces under Gen. William S. Rosecrans clashed with Confederates under Gen. Sterling Price. The battle is famous in military history for an **\"acoustic shadow\"**\u2014atmospheric conditions and topography prevented Union General Ulysses S. Grant, who was just a few miles away, from hearing the sounds of the gunfire, keeping his reinforcements from joining the fight in time.\n\n### 3. Key Attractions & Things to Do\n* **Mineral Springs Park:** Located in downtown Iuka, this park is built around natural mineral springs. At the 1904 World's Fair in St. Louis, water from these springs actually won the award for the **\"cleanest and best mineral water in the world.\"** Today, the park features walking tracks, a historic dogtrot log cabin, and pavilions.\n* **J.P. Coleman State Park & Pickwick Lake:** Situated just north of the town along the Tennessee River, this area is a major hub for outdoor recreation, including boating, fishing, hiking, and camping.\n* **The Old Courthouse Museum:** Built in 1870, this historic building displays regional Civil War relics, Native American artifacts, and local history.\n* **Downtown Iuka:** The downtown area is a classic, preserved Southern main street featuring historic architecture, local boutiques, and antique shops.",
